<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arthur Phillip</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1738">1738</a>–<a href="/years/1814">1814</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Arthur Phillip was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> officer who commanded the First Fleet of convict ships to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>. He founded the settlement at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sydney-cove">Sydney Cove</a> in January 1788 and served as the first governor of New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-wales">South Wales</a> until 1792, guiding the colony through its early food shortages.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arthur-phillip.jpg" alt="Arthur Phillip" width="320" height="376" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Francis Wheatley · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arthur_Phillip_-_Wheatley_ML124_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Arthur Phillip was born on 11 October 1738 in the parish of All Hallows, Bread Street, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. His father, Jacob Phillip, an immigrant from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a>, is variously described as a language teacher, a ship owner, a merchant captain or a common seaman, and died in 1739. His mother, Elizabeth Breach, was the widow of a seaman. From 1751 to 1753 he attended the Greenwich Hospital School, a charity school for the sons of poor seafarers, where he learned literacy, arithmetic and navigation, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cartography">cartography</a>. He then became an apprentice on the whaling ship Fortune, which hunted whales near Svalbard, before enlisting in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> in 1755.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Phillip served under his mother's cousin, Captain Michael Everitt, on HMS Buckingham, HMS Union and HMS Stirling Castle during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a>, including the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menorca">Minorca</a> in 1756 and the Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a>. He became a lieutenant on 7 June 1761 and went on half-pay in 1763. He later served in the Portuguese Navy against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, was recalled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> in 1778 and, from 1784 to 1786, was employed by the Home Office official Evan Nepean to report on French naval arsenals such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulon">Toulon</a>.</p><p>In 1786 he was appointed to command the First Fleet of eleven ships, which was to found a penal colony in New South Wales. Finding Botany Bay unsuitable, he settled at Port Jackson. The flag was raised on 26 January 1788, and his government was formally inaugurated on 7 February. Phillip had asked for trained convicts but received only twelve carpenters and a few men with farming knowledge, and he faced severe problems of labour, discipline and supply. He set up civil courts that applied to everyone, convicts included, and clashed with marine officers under Major Robert Ross who refused any duty but guard duty.</p><p>His orders were to live in amity with Aboriginal people, and he treated them as under the protection of British law. The Eora avoided the settlement in its first year, attacked colonists who trespassed on their lands, and cultural differences led eventually to conflict. When Phillip sailed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> on 11 December 1792 for treatment of kidney stones, the colony had land grants, systematic farming and a water supply.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Phillip resigned the governorship on medical advice. After recovering he returned to naval duty in 1796, commanded the Hampshire Sea Fencibles, retired from active service in 1805 and rose to Admiral of the Blue on 4 June 1814. He died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bath">Bath</a> on 31 August 1814. Port Phillip, Phillip Island and Phillip Street in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sydney-nova-scotia">Sydney</a> bear his name, and memorials stand in Bath Abbey, near Saint Paul's Cathedral in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and in Sydney's Royal Botanic Garden.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Arthur Phillip” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Captain <b>Arthur Phillip</b> established a penal colony at Sydney Cove with some 1,300 convicts, marines and officials.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-26">26 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Eleven ships under Captain <b>Arthur Phillip</b> left England carrying some 1,400 people, about half of them convicts.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-13">13 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After losing the American colonies as a destination for convicts, Britain sent the First Fleet under <b>Arthur Phillip</b>, which landed at Sydney Cove in January 1788.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-new-south-wales">Colony of New South Wales</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Bath, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1787" aria-label="London on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4547 381 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Arthur Phillip"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4997 651h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4997 651h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1787">What was here in 1787? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
